+<div class="wrap">
+  <div class="eyebrow">City Edit · Intersection Danger Audit</div>
+  <h1>Where NYC streets hurt people — right now</h1>
+  <div class="dateline">
+    Crash data through <strong>June 11, 2026</strong> (freshest NYPD release) · analysis run July 27, 2026 ·
+    supersedes the 2023-on pooled analysis in <em>NYC Intersection Data Analysis</em> (PDF)
+  </div>
+  <hr class="rule">
+
+  <p>
+    The previous top-20 pooled 3½ years of crashes. That was the right way to find chronically bad
+    intersections — and the wrong way to decide where to hang a poster <em>today</em>, because the city keeps
+    responding to exactly this data. This audit re-pulls the full
+    NYPD Motor Vehicle Collisions feed, keeps every crash since 2023 that injured or killed a pedestrian or
+    cyclist, snaps each one to a named street intersection in City Edit's own walk graph (so every ranked
+    intersection deep-links into the app), and re-ranks under six different definitions of "dangerous."
+  </p>
+
+  <div class="stats">
+    <div class="stat"><div class="v">11,710</div><div class="l">ped/cyclist crashes, last 12 months</div></div>
+    <div class="stat"><div class="v">7,570</div><div class="l">pedestrians injured, last 12 months</div></div>
+    <div class="stat"><div class="v red">88</div><div class="l">pedestrians killed, last 12 months</div></div>
+    <div class="stat"><div class="v">4,500</div><div class="l">cyclists injured, last 12 months</div></div>
+    <div class="stat"><div class="v red">19</div><div class="l">cyclists killed, last 12 months</div></div>
+    <div class="stat"><div class="v">16,253</div><div class="l">intersections with ≥1 such crash since 2023</div></div>
+  </div>
+
+  <h2><span class="no">1.</span> The old top-20 is stale — measurably</h2>
+  <p>
+    Re-rank the PDF's top 20 on <b>the last 12 months only</b> and just three stay in the citywide top 50.
+    Chrystie St / Grand St — #7 all-time — recorded <b>3</b> crashes in the past year against a historical pace
+    of ~8/yr and falls to #504. 8th Ave / W 57th St falls to #1048. Meanwhile E Fordham Rd / Webster Ave more
+    than doubled its own baseline. Pooled rankings memorialize where danger <em>was</em>; the freshness-weighted
+    rankings below track where it <em>is</em>.
+  </p>
+  <div class="tblwrap"><table id="pdfTable" aria-label="PDF top 20 re-ranked on fresh data"></table></div>
+
+  <h2><span class="no">2.</span> Six definitions of "most dangerous"</h2>
+  <p>
+    "Dangerous" is a modeling choice, and different choices target different posters. Each definition below is
+    computed for every intersection; the tabs show the citywide top 10 under each.
+  </p>
+  <div class="defs">
+    <div class="def"><h4><span class="tag">A</span> Chronic (the PDF's definition)</h4>
+      <p>Raw count of ped/cyclist injury-or-death crashes, 2023 → now. Finds the persistent offenders; blind to fixes.</p></div>
+    <div class="def"><h4><span class="tag">B</span> Fresh &amp; severity-weighted</h4>
+      <p>Last 12 months only, <code>injured + 8×killed</code>. The headline "most dangerous right now" number. The 8× is a choice — deaths dominate but a single fatal crash can't be outrun by two injuries.</p></div>
+    <div class="def"><h4><span class="tag">C</span> Recency-decayed</h4>
+      <p>Every crash counts, weighted <code>0.5^(age/12mo)</code>. Smoother than a hard 12-month cut; a fix shows up as a fading score within months. <b>Recommended default for poster placement.</b></p></div>
+    <div class="def"><h4><span class="tag">D</span> Mode-specific (ped / cyclist)</h4>
+      <p>Definitions B–C restricted to pedestrian or cyclist victims. The two maps barely overlap — cyclist danger lives on bridge approaches and avenue bike routes, pedestrian danger on wide crossing distances.</p></div>
+    <div class="def"><h4><span class="tag">E</span> Heating up (trend outlier)</h4>
+      <p>Poisson surprise: how improbable is the last-12-month count given the intersection's own 2023→mid-2025 rate (+½ pseudo-crash prior)? Catches emerging hotspots a pooled rank won't see for years.</p></div>
+    <div class="def"><h4><span class="tag">F</span> After dark</h4>
+      <p>Share of crashes between 9pm and 6am (among intersections with ≥4 fresh crashes). A different intervention story: lighting, visibility, late-night speeds.</p></div>
+  </div>
+
+  <div class="tabs" role="tablist" id="tabbar"></div>
+  <p class="tabnote" id="tabnote"></p>
+  <div class="tblwrap"><table id="topTable" aria-label="Top intersections under selected definition"></table></div>
+
+  <div class="chart">
+    <p class="t">Then vs. now — the recency-decayed top 12</p>
+    <p class="s">Annualized crash pace, 2023 → mid-2025 baseline vs. the actual last 12 months. Persistent hotspots keep pace; new ones outrun their own history.</p>
+    <div class="legend">
+      <span><span class="sw" style="background:var(--series-base)"></span>baseline pace (crashes/yr)</span>
+      <span><span class="sw" style="background:var(--series-fresh)"></span>last 12 months (crashes)</span>
+    </div>
+    <div id="chartBars"></div>
+  </div>
+
+  <h2><span class="no">3.</span> Outliers: who's escaping their own history</h2>
+  <p>
+    Every intersection with real signal, plotted against itself: historical pace (x) vs. the last 12 months (y).
+    On the dashed line, nothing changed. Above it and improbably so (Poisson surprise ≥ 3, i.e. p &lt; 0.001):
+    <b style="color:var(--heat)">heating up</b>. Far below with a big baseline:
+    <b style="color:var(--cool)">cooling</b> — several of these sit exactly where DOT shipped redesigns,
+    which is the clearest evidence in the data that the city does respond and stale rankings do go stale.
+  </p>
+  <div class="chart">
+    <p class="t">Baseline pace vs. last 12 months, per intersection</p>
+    <p class="s">998 intersections with ≥3 fresh crashes or ≥8 baseline crashes. Hover any point.</p>
+    <div class="legend">
+      <span><span class="sw dot" style="background:var(--heat)"></span>heating up (surprise ≥ 3)</span>
+      <span><span class="sw dot" style="background:var(--cool)"></span>cooling (surprise ≥ 1.5)</span>
+      <span><span class="sw dot" style="background:var(--stable); opacity:.55"></span>tracking baseline</span>
+    </div>
+    <div id="chartScatter"></div>
+  </div>
+
+  <div class="chart">
+    <p class="t">Danger is a long tail</p>
+    <p class="s">Distribution of the fresh severity-weighted score (definition B) across all 16,253 intersections — log scale. The poster campaign targets the far right of this curve; the neighborhood-#1 threshold (score ≥ 3) already excludes 92% of intersections.</p>
+    <div id="chartDist"></div>
+  </div>
+
+  <h2><span class="no">4.</span> Poster targeting</h2>
+  <p>
+    Recommended placement tiers, each mapping to a poster family. Every target carries a QR deep link of the form
+    <code class="path">https://cityedit.org/m/nyc-walkways?w=&lt;lat&gt;,&lt;lon&gt;</code> — the phone opens City Edit
+    with that exact intersection selected, ready to vote.
+  </p>
+  <div class="tiers">
+    <div class="tier"><h4>Citywide #1 posters (4 designs from the PDF, replicated)</h4>
+      <p>"#1 most dangerous for pedestrians in NYC" → <b>Amsterdam Ave / W 109th St</b> (2 killed, 3 injured in 12 months).
+      "#1 for cyclists" → <b>Frederick Douglass Blvd / W 125th St</b> (top cyclist recency score; a cyclist killed March 2026).
+      Strongest claims, few sites.</p></div>
+    <div class="tier"><h4>Neighborhood #1 posters (novel design, ~150 sites)</h4>
+      <p>"The most dangerous intersection in <b>Melrose</b>" — one per NTA-2020 neighborhood with score ≥ 3.
+      This is the PDF's own "Alternatively:" idea made procedural; 184 neighborhoods qualify today, top 15 listed below.</p></div>
+    <div class="tier"><h4>Heating-up posters (novel design)</h4>
+      <p>"This intersection is heating up" for trend outliers — e.g. <b>Pelham Pkwy S / White Plains Rd</b> (1.4/yr baseline → 11 crashes).
+      These are the places the chronic list won't flag for years, and where a resident's "wait, it got bad here" intuition is right.</p></div>
+    <div class="tier"><h4>After-dark posters (novel design)</h4>
+      <p>Night-share outliers get the dark variant ("most dangerous at night"). Different message, same QR mechanics.</p></div>
+  </div>
+  <h3>Neighborhood #1s — top 15 by fresh weighted score</h3>
+  <div class="tblwrap"><table id="ntaTable" aria-label="Per-neighborhood most dangerous intersections"></table></div>
+
+  <h2><span class="no">5.</span> Method &amp; honest caveats</h2>
+  <p>
+    <b>Join.</b> 47,965 crash rows pulled (Socrata <code class="path">h9gi-nx95</code>); 2,251 lacked usable coordinates; 5,611
+    fell &gt;40 m from any named intersection (mostly mid-block) and are excluded; 40,103 snapped to 16,253 intersections.
+    Intersections are nodes in City Edit's NYC walk graph where ≥2 distinct street names meet — the same graph the app serves,
+    so poster QR coordinates resolve to selectable nodes.
+    <b>Weights.</b> The 8× death weight and 12-month half-life are dials, not truths; rankings above are shown under multiple
+    settings precisely so the choice is visible. <b>Denominators.</b> No exposure normalization — a Midtown intersection sees
+    100× the foot traffic of a residential one, so these are counts of harm, not per-crossing risk. That's the right metric for
+    "where should a poster hang," the wrong one for "which design is most defective."
+    <b>Data lag.</b> NYPD publishes with ~6-week delay; "last 12 months" means through June 11, 2026. Late-filed reports can
+    still top up recent months slightly. <b>Underreporting.</b> Cyclist-only falls and near-misses never enter this dataset —
+    which is exactly the gap the posters ask people to fill in City Edit.
+  </p>
+
+  <div class="foot">
+    <p>Sources: NYC Open Data — Motor Vehicle Collisions (Crashes), pulled 2026-07-27 · NTA 2020 boundaries · City Edit NYC walk graph (2026-06-17 build).
+    Reproduce: <code class="path">scripts/build_intersection_rankings.py</code> → <code class="path">data/analysis/*.csv</code>.
+    Posters: <code class="path">scripts/generate_posters.py</code> · replicas &amp; templates in <code class="path">data/posters/</code>.</p>
+  </div>
+</div>
+<div id="tip" role="status"></div>
